        <h1>Chapter 26</h1>

        <p>Lisa drove in silence, figuring out what she was going to say. She ran through the facts in her head, organizing them the exact same way she would prep for an interrogation.</p>

        <p>Becky was going to scream, cry, deflect, and use guilt. Lisa was ready for all of it.</p>

        <p>She parked in the driveway, cut the engine, and grabbed the bag.</p>

        <p>She unlocked the front door and pushed it open. The house was quiet. She stepped in and looked toward the living room.</p>

        <p>Becky was sitting in the chair. She had the shoulder sling on, her casted leg propped up on the ottoman. A mug of coffee on the end table.</p>

        <p>Becky stared at the front door. She had sadness and anger in her face.</p>

        <p>“So,” Becky said, sounding like a smart ass. “You decided to finally come back.”</p>

        <p>Lisa walked into the center of the living room. She let the bag slide off her shoulder, dropping it onto the floor. “Where is Betsy?” Lisa asked, keeping her voice calm.</p>

        <p>“She’s upstairs,” Becky said. “She is a wreck, Lisa. Because her mother lied to her face, disappeared for an entire night, and ignored us while I sat here injured and terrified. Do you have any idea what you put us through?”</p>

        <p>Lisa stood there, looking at the woman she had spent the last thirteen years with. There was no affection left. There wasn't even pity.</p>

        <p>“Keep your voice down,” Lisa said. “We are going to have a conversation, and we are going to keep it quiet so you don't drag Betsy into this.”</p>

        <p>Becky’s eyes grew angrier. She didn't like the tone. Lisa wasn't apologizing. Lisa wasn't begging for forgiveness or acting guilty.</p>

        <p>“You don't get to walk in here and give me orders,” Becky said. “You lied about going to the precinct. You left me here helpless. You owe me an explanation, and you owe your daughter an apology.”</p>

        <p>Lisa bent down. She unzipped the main compartment of the bag and reached inside.</p>

        <p>She pulled out the stack of papers she had found in the master closet.</p>

        <p>She stood up, walked to the chair, and dropped the stack directly onto the glass coffee table, right in front of Becky.</p>

        <p>The bank logos, the private account numbers, the personal loan applications, all of it was sitting in plain sight.</p>

        <p>Lisa watched Becky's face. She watched the anger vanish instantly, replaced by a flash of panic.</p>

        <p>Becky stared down at the documents.</p>

        <p>“I was looking for my travel bag in the closet,” Lisa said. “Thirteen years, Becky. Thirteen years of you siphoning my paycheck out of our joint account. Thirteen years of personal loans taken out in my name. Forgery. Wire fraud. Identity theft.”</p>

        <p>Becky started breathing heavier. She scrambled for an excuse. “You… you don't understand finances, Lisa. You never have. I was setting up a secure future for us. For Betsy’s college.”</p>

        <p>“Do not lie to me,” Lisa said, stepping closer to the table. “You took out a second mortgage on this house and forged my signature. You have thousands of dollars sitting in an account that only has your name on it. This wasn't a savings plan. This was an exit strategy, and it was a trap.”</p>

        <p>Becky looked up. The victim act completely dissolved into pure malice.</p>

        <p>“You work a dangerous job!” Becky said, leaning forward. “You could get shot on a call! I had to protect myself and Betsy! I had to make sure we weren't left with nothing if you decided to play the hero and get yourself killed!”</p>

        <p>“You stole from me,” Lisa said. “You stole my wages, you ruined my credit, and you sat in that guest room for a month demanding I wait on you hand and foot because you are obsessed with control.”</p>

        <p>Lisa rested her hands on her hips. She looked down at Becky.</p>

        <p>“Here is what is going to happen right now,” Lisa said. “I am going to a lawyer. I am having divorce papers drawn up. You are going to sign them.”</p>

        <p>Becky let out a laugh. “You think you can just throw me out? Half of everything is mine. If you file for divorce, I will take you for alimony, I will take this house, and I will take Betsy.”</p>

        <p>“If you fight me on a single clause in those papers,” Lisa said, leaning down so she was right at eye level with Becky, “I am taking this stack of bank statements straight to the District Attorney. I will file a formal report for felony fraud. They will freeze every single asset you have, they will seize that private account, and you will spend the next ten years in a federal prison.”</p>

        <p>Becky stared at her.</p>

        <p>“I don't want the money,” Lisa said. “You can keep every single cent you stole. I will walk away with absolutely nothing. But in exchange, you are signing over full custody of Betsy to me. You are taking your stolen money, you are packing your bags, and you are leaving.”</p>

        <p>Lisa stared at her sitting in the chair, watching the panic in Becky's eyes. It was a pathetic sight.</p>

        <p>"Did you ever love me?" Lisa asked. "Or was it the money? You figured out an easy mark, someone who saved a lot before they met you... worked double shifts and trusted you completely, so you kept me around just to fund your private escape plan?"</p>

        <p>Becky’s face turned red. The tears fell. She reached out with her hand, trying to grab Lisa's jacket.</p>

        <p>"How can you even ask me that?" Becky cried. "Of course I loved you! I still love you, Lisa! Please, don't do this. I made a mistake. I was just so scared of being left alone if something happened to you on the job. I panicked. It was stupid, I know it was stupid, but I did it for us!"</p>

        <p>Lisa took a step back, out of reach. She looked at the tears, feeling absolutely nothing.</p>

        <p>"You didn't do it for us," Lisa said. "You did it for yourself. You forged my name on a second mortgage. You stole from my daughter's future."</p>

        <p>"I'll give it back!" Becky begged, leaning forward, ignoring the supposedly agonizing pain in her collarbone. "I'll transfer every single cent back into the joint account today. I'll put your name on everything. Just please, Lisa, don't throw our marriage away over money. Think of Betsy. You're going to destroy our family."</p>

        <p>"You destroyed this family years ago," Lisa said, pissed off. "And don't try to use Betsy as a shield. Not anymore. You've been manipulating her to punish me, I'm putting a stop to it today."</p>

        <p>"I'm injured!" Becky cried. "You can't throw me out on the street! I can't even dress myself! Where am I supposed to go?"</p>

        <p>"You have hundreds of thousands of dollars," Lisa said. "You can hire a nurse. And a driver. And don't play the helpless invalid with me anymore, Becky. I know you can move your arm."</p>

        <p>The tears stopped instantly. Her anger came back.</p>

        <p>"You're a miserable bitch," Becky shouted. "You think you're so smart. You think you can just walk in here and dictate the terms? You think you can just take my daughter?"</p>

        <p>"She's my daughter," Lisa said quietly. "And you are going to sign the papers giving me full custody, or you are going to prison. There is no negotiation."</p>

        <p>Becky was pissed. She grabbed the coffee mug off the table and threw it as hard as she could directly at Lisa's head.</p>

        <p>Lisa ducked. The mug flew past her ear and slammed into the wall near the front door, breaking into pieces and splashing coffee down it.</p>

        <p>Betsy heard the noise and ran out of her room, stopping at the top of the stairs. Listening.</p>

        <p>Lisa slowly turned her head back to face Becky. She didn't flinch. She didn't yell. The violence was just the final confirmation she needed.</p>

        <p>"Tell me Lisa, did you ever love me?" Becky said, mocking Lisa's earlier question. "Or did you, right up until you slept with that slut next door?"</p>

        <p>The words hung in the air. The truth finally out into the open. She knows.</p>

        <p>Lisa just stood there.</p>

        <p>"Carla is not a slut!" Lisa said. She stepped closer to the chair, looking down at Becky. "She is ten times the woman you ever were. She's the love of my life, and I only wish I had found her sooner."</p>

        <p>Becky let out a laugh in disbelief. "Love of your life. Right. You’ve known her how long?"</p>

        <p>"Long enough to know what I’ve been missing my whole life," Lisa fired back, her voice shaking with anger. "You twisted fuck."</p>

        <p>Becky sat back slightly against the chair. Pissed she wasn’t getting to Lisa this time.</p>

        <p>"Tell you what, Becky," Lisa said. "I'm gonna call the hospital and get you a nurse. She'll come take care of you until that cast comes off and you can walk on your own two feet. Because I am going to leave this house. I'm going to take Betsy, and you are going to figure out a place to stay. I am going to look out for what's best for our daughter, and it will not be you, Becky."</p>

        <p>Lisa didn't wait for a response. She bent down, grabbed the bank statements off the table, and shoved them back into her bag.</p>

        <p>Betsy ran quietly back in her room. Horrified at what she just heard.</p>

        <p>Lisa turned and walked toward the staircase. Lisa took them two at a time. She walked down the hall and knocked on Betsy’s closed door.</p>

        <p>There was no answer, just the sound of crying. Lisa turned the knob and pushed the door open.</p>

        <p>Betsy was sitting on the carpet floor right in front of her bed, her knees pulled tight to her chest, her face buried in her arms.</p>

        <p>She was crying, and it broke Lisa.</p>

        <p>Lisa dropped her bag in the doorway and rushed over. She dropped to her knees on the floor next to her daughter.</p>

        <p>"Hey, hey, what's wrong, Betsy?" Lisa said. She wrapped her arms around her, pulling her into a tight hug.</p>

        <p>"Why is Mom doing this?" Betsy cried in her mom’s shoulder, her whole body shaking.</p>

        <p>"Doing what, baby?" Lisa asked, her hand smoothing down the back of Betsy's hair.</p>

        <p>"I heard everything, Mom..." Betsy sobbed, pulling back enough to look at Lisa with red, swollen eyes.</p>

        <p>Lisa's heart broke. "Oh, sweetie... you weren't meant to hear that. I'm so sorry."</p>

        <p>"Mom, she stole from you?" Betsy asked. She wiped her nose. "And... what she said about Carla?"</p>

        <p>Lisa closed her eyes for a second, the weight of the last few months crashing down on her all at once.</p>

        <p>She didn't want to dump all of this on a sixteen-year-old, but the secret was out, and lying to Betsy now would only do more damage.</p>

        <p>Lisa looked at her daughter. "It's complicated," Lisa said softly, squeezing Betsy's shoulders. "Please, just pack a suitcase, okay? We are going to just go somewhere for a few days until we work this out. Yea? We are going to go to a hotel, and once we get there, we will talk about anything you want to. I will answer every single question you have, I promise. But right now, we just need to get out of this house."</p>

        <p>Betsy looked at the exhaustion on her mom’s face and nodded. She sniffled, wiping her cheeks with the back of her hand, and stood up to pull a bag out of her closet.</p>

        <p>"I'll be right across the hall," Lisa promised, standing up. "Five minutes."</p>

        <p>Lisa walked out of the room, she went into her bedroom. She grabbed some clothes, boots, and the rest of her toiletries, shoving them into her bag.</p>

        <p>She pulled her phone out of her pocket. She texted Carla.</p>

        <p><em>It's done. I'm taking Betsy and going back to the hotel.</em></p>

        <p>She hit send. She responded quickly.</p>

        <p><em>I hope you're ok? Please let me know if you need me.</em></p>

        <p>Lisa stared at the text. She did need her. But Betsy deserved answers.</p>

        <p>Lisa and Betsy walked down the stairs. As they reached the bottom step, Betsy paused.</p>

        <p>She turned her head and looked into the living room at Becky, who was still sitting in the chair.</p>

        <p>Betsy looked at her mother, trying to figure out what happened.</p>

        <p>Becky didn't apologize. She just stared back at them.</p>

        <p>"Leave," Becky said, her tone nasty and bitter. "You always liked her better anyways."</p>

        <p>The words hit Betsy hard; she started crying again.</p>

        <p>Lisa stepped up behind her daughter. She put her hand on Betsy’s shoulder.</p>

        <p>"Come on," Lisa said.</p>

        <p>She gently pushed Betsy toward the front door, letting her walk out to the porch first.</p>

        <p>Lisa stayed behind for a second. She turned her head and looked at Becky one last time.</p>

        <p>"Enjoy what you've taken from us," Lisa said. “You have one week to be gone. Figure it out, or I'm turning you in."</p>

        <p>Lisa turned around and walked out. She pulled the door shut slowly.</p>

        <p>As she stood on the porch, a weight lifted off her chest. She took a deep breath. She walked down the steps, got into the driver’s seat of her car, and started the engine.</p>

        <p>Inside the house, the second the engine turned over, Becky stood up.</p>

        <p>She didn't use the crutches. She didn't favor her leg. She stood up straight, walked over to the front window, and pulled the blinds back.</p>

        <p>She watched Lisa’s car back out of the driveway and pull away down the street.</p>

        <p>Becky’s gaze shifted to the house next door. She stared at Carla’s property, angry.</p>

        <p>In the car, the drive was silent. Lisa kept her eyes on the road, giving Betsy space to process.</p>

        <p>Betsy sat in the passenger seat, staring out the window as tears fell down her cheeks.</p>

        <p>Lisa reached across the center console and placed her hand over Betsy’s knee, giving it a squeeze.</p>

        <p>Betsy turned her head, wiping her eyes, and managed a small smile at her mom.</p>

        <p>Ten minutes later, Lisa pulled into the parking lot of the Marriott. She grabbed both of their bags from the back, slinging her duffel over her shoulder, and walked Betsy through the lobby to the elevators.</p>

        <p>When Lisa unlocked the door to her suite on the top floor, she held her breath for a second.</p>

        <p>But as she pushed the door open, she let out a sigh of relief. Room service had already been through. The bed was made with fresh linens, the towels were replaced, and the wreck she and Carla had made of the place the night before was completely erased.</p>

        <p>"Wow," Betsy said, looking around the large room and the balcony. "This is nice."</p>

        <p>"Drop your bag anywhere," Lisa said, setting hers down by the closet. "Are you hungry? Thirsty?"</p>

        <p>"No, I'm okay," Betsy said. She walked over to the small couch and sat down. She pulled her knees up, wrapping her arms around her legs.</p>

        <p>Lisa walked over and sat down next to her, facing her daughter.</p>

        <p>"So," Betsy finally said. "You said you would answer my questions."</p>

        <p>"I did," Lisa agreed. "Whatever you want to know, Bets. I won't lie to you."</p>

        <p>Betsy took a slow breath. "What did she steal?"</p>

        <p>"Money," Lisa said simply. "A lot of it. I found a box of bank statements hidden in the back of our closet when I went to get my bag yesterday. She has been taking money out of my paychecks and putting it into a private account that only has her name on it."</p>

        <p>Betsy frowned, trying to make the math work in her head. "But you guys share a bank account. How did she do that without you noticing?"</p>

        <p>"She was taking out personal loans in my name to cover the deficits," Lisa explained. "And she took out a second mortgage on our house. She forged my signature on the paperwork. She's been doing it for thirteen years."</p>

        <p>Betsy just stared at her. "Thirteen years? Since I was three?"</p>

        <p>"Yeah."</p>

        <p>"Why?" Betsy asked, getting emotional. "Did she want to leave us?"</p>

        <p>"No," Lisa said, shaking her head. "I don't think she ever intended to leave. Becky is… Becky needs to be in control, Betsy. Of everything. She needs to know that she holds all the cards. By taking the money and putting me in debt, she made sure that I could never afford to leave her. It was an insurance policy. It was a way to keep me trapped."</p>

        <p>Betsy looked down at her hands, processing the scale of the manipulation. "Is that why she was acting so crazy about her injuries?”</p>

        <p>"Yes," Lisa said. "I think so…When she fell down those stairs, she realized it forced me to stay home. It forced me to wait on her, to feel guilty."</p>

        <p>"Did she fake the fall?" Betsy asked, looking up.</p>

        <p>"I don't know if she threw herself down the stairs on purpose," Lisa admitted honestly.</p>

        <p>"But she definitely faked the recovery. You saw her reaching for that book. The doctors told us weeks ago that she should be doing physical therapy and walking around. She chose to stay in that bed just to keep me tied to it."</p>

        <p>Betsy let out a breath. "She tried to tell me you were abandoning us. She cried to me all week. She told me you hated us."</p>

        <p>"I know," Lisa said, her chest aching. "And I am so incredibly sorry you were put in the middle of that. She saw that her guilt trips weren't working on me anymore, so she pivoted and used you to get to me. That voicemail she had you leave last night? That was the end of the line for me. I can handle her messing with my head, but I will not let her mess with yours."</p>

        <p>Betsy nodded slowly. She pulled a loose thread on the knee of her jeans, clearly working up the courage for the next topic.</p>

        <p>"So," Betsy said, keeping her eyes focused on the thread. "Carla."</p>

        <p>Lisa didn't tense up. She didn't look away. "Carla."</p>

        <p>"Mom said you were sleeping with her," Betsy said, finally looking her mom in the eye. "Is it true?"</p>

        <p>"Yes," Lisa said. No hesitation. No defensive lies.</p>

        <p>Betsy just watched her. "How long?"</p>

        <p>"Awhile," Lisa answered.</p>

        <p>"While Mom was stuck in the bed?" Betsy asked, the question carrying a little teenage judgment.</p>

        <p>"Yes," Lisa said. She took a breath. "I'm not going to sit here and tell you that having an affair is a good thing, Betsy. It's a massive betrayal of trust in a marriage. If things were normal between your mother and me, what I did would make me the villain of this story."</p>

        <p>"But things weren't normal," Betsy stated.</p>

        <p>"No, they weren't," Lisa agreed. "I was drowning in that house. I was working double shifts just to pay the bills, and coming home to someone who constantly made me feel like I was failing her. I felt completely empty. And then Carla moved in next door."</p>

        <p>Lisa paused, making sure she had her words right. "I didn't plan it, Bets. I didn't go looking for someone to cheat with. But Carla... Carla actually saw me. She listened to me. She didn't demand anything from me. It just started as talking about stuff, and then it turned into something else. Something real."</p>

        <p>"Are you in love with her?" Betsy asked directly.</p>

        <p>"Yes," Lisa said. "Very much."</p>

        <p>Betsy absorbed that. She leaned back against the couch cushions, looking at the television on the wall. "Are you divorcing Mom because of Carla?"</p>

        <p>"No," Lisa said, needing Betsy to understand the distinction. "I am divorcing your mother because she has been financially and emotionally abusing me for over a decade. I am divorcing her because she manipulated you. Even if Carla had never moved in next door, finding those bank statements today would have ended the marriage anyway. Carla isn't the reason I'm leaving. Carla is just the person I want to be with now that I am."</p>

        <p>"Mom is going to make this horrible," Betsy warned. "She's going to fight you."</p>

        <p>"I gave her a choice before we left," Lisa explained. "I told her she has one week to pack her bags and leave. She signs the divorce papers and she gives me full custody of you. If she does that, I let her keep all the money she stole, and I don't report her to the police."</p>

        <p>Betsy’s eyes went wide. "You're letting her keep it? But it's your money!"</p>

        <p>"It's just money, Betsy," Lisa said, shaking her head. "If I try to fight her for it, we will be stuck in court for two years. She will drag you through a custody battle. She will play the victim to a judge. I don't want to fight her. I just want out. Buying our freedom is worth every single cent in that account."</p>

        <p>Betsy sat quietly for a long time. The maturity in the sixteen-year-old was surprising to Lisa.</p>

        <p>She was processing the collapse of her family, the reality of her mother's crimes, and her other mother's affair, all in the span of twenty minutes.</p>

        <p>"Are we going to have to move?" Betsy finally asked.</p>

        <p>"No," Lisa said. "She has one week to leave the house. Once she's out, we will go back home. You don't have to change schools. You don't have to change your life. This isn’t your fault.”</p>

        <p>"And Carla?" Betsy asked.</p>

        <p>"Carla lives right next door," Lisa smiled. "We are going to take things one step at a time. Right now, my only priority is making sure you feel safe and that you know what's going on."</p>

        <p>Betsy nodded. She let her legs drop down from the couch. She looked at her mom, the exhaustion finally catching up to her.</p>

        <p>"I don't hate you for the Carla thing," Betsy said quietly.</p>

        <p>That made Lisa smile.</p>

        <p>"Mom was awful to you," Betsy continued, honest. "I saw it. I saw how tired you looked every single day. When Carla found me crying on the street yesterday and invited me in... she was just really nice. She gave me tea. She felt safe. I get why you like her."</p>

        <p>Lisa reached out, grabbing Betsy’s hand and pulling her toward her. Lisa held her tight, burying her face in her daughter's shoulder.</p>
        
        <p>"Thank you, Bets," Lisa whispered, her voice thick. "I love you so much."</p>

        <p>"I love you too, Mom," Betsy said, hugging her back. "What do we do now?"</p>

        <p>Lisa pulled back, keeping her hands on Betsy's arms. She smiled.</p>

        <p>"Now," Lisa said, "we order a ridiculous amount of room service, we put a movie on, and we sleep through the rest of the day.”</p>

        <p>Lisa stood at the foot of the bed, watching Betsy sleep.</p>

        <p>She had passed out hard. A half-eaten plate of room service sandwiches sat on the table.</p>

        <p>Lisa pulled the curtains closed. She slid the glass open and stepped out on the patio.</p>

        <p>She walked over to the patio couch and sat down, then pulled her phone from her pocket.</p>

        <p>She dialed Carla’s number.</p>

        <p>Carla answered on the very first ring. "Lisa."</p>
        
        <p>Just hearing her voice made the tight muscles in Lisa’s neck instantly release.</p>

        <p>“Hey you.” Lisa said.</p>

        <p>“Is everything ok? How is your daughter?” Carla asked. She was so concerned.</p>

        <p>"She’s asleep," Lisa said. "She ate half a sandwich and passed out hard. It’s been a rough day."</p>

        <p>"I bet," Carla said. "Are you guys ok at the hotel? Do you have everything you need?"</p>

        <p>"Yeah, we're good," Lisa said. She closed her eyes, letting the reality of the day finally sink in. "It's done, she’s out of my life."</p>

        <p>"Tell me everything," Carla asked.</p>

        <p>Lisa recounted the entire confrontation, going through the details.</p>

        <p>"She tried to play the victim first," Lisa explained. "She cried. She told me she did it for us, to protect the family in case I got killed on the job. When I didn't buy it, she completely flipped. She showed exactly who she is. It was like a switch flipped in her head."</p>

        <p>"What did she do?" Carla asked.</p>

        <p>"She threw a coffee mug right at my head," Lisa said. "It smashed into the wall by the front door. Betsy was upstairs and heard the crash. She heard the yelling."</p>

        <p>"Jesus Christ, Lisa," Carla said, shocked. "Did it hit you?"</p>

        <p>"No, I ducked it," Lisa said. "But that was the end of it. The violence was the absolute last straw. I told her she has one week to pack her bags and get out, or I'm taking the file to the DA and she's going to federal prison. I grabbed Betsy, and we walked out."</p>

        <p>"I hate that I wasn't there,” she said.</p>

        <p>"I'm fine," Lisa promised. "But you need to listen to me, Carla. She is completely backed into a corner right now, and she is dangerous. She has no leverage left, and she knows it. I am not sure what she is capable of right now. I’m worried for you to be next door.”</p>

        <p>"You don't have to worry about that," Carla said. "I'm not at the house. I drove into the city this afternoon. I had a meeting with my publisher, and I decided to just stay at my apartment here for the weekend to get some writing done. I am miles away from her."</p>

        <p>Lisa closed her eyes with a wave of relief.</p>

        <p>"Good. Stay there."</p>

        <p>"I will," Carla said. "But Lisa... since Betsy heard the yelling... how is she doing? Are you two okay?"</p>

        <p>Lisa smiled. "We're okay. We had a long talk when we got here. I didn't hide anything. I told her the truth about the money, and I told her the truth about you and me."</p>

        <p>"Does she hate me? Does she blame me for breaking up the family?" Carla asked.</p>

        <p>"No," Lisa said immediately. "Carla, listen to me. Betsy told me she gets it. She said Becky was awful to me, and she saw how tired I was every single day. And then she talked about seeing you and talking to you that day."</p>

        <p>"Oh?" Carla asked.</p>

        <p>"You gave her a safe place to sit when her house felt like a war zone," Lisa said. "Betsy said you were really nice. She said you made her feel safe. She doesn't hate you, Carla. She actually likes you. She was very mature about the whole thing."</p>

        <p>“Oh, well that’s good, I just feel that I caused all this Lisa...” Carla said.</p>

